---
/**
 * /faq — Frequently Asked Questions  (FAQ brief §2, §3, §5, §12, §13)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * This route used to render a reservation page. It is now the real thing: the
 * page a buyer reads BEFORE submitting a request, answering the practical
 * questions about MOQ, suppliers, samples, customisation, purchasing, quality
 * control, shipping, fees, and whether they need to be a company at all.
 *
 * STRUCTURE (brief §15)
 *   01 Hero                    §2   eyebrow, H1, supporting text, two actions
 *   02 Category navigation     §3   nine entries, in-page
 *   03 Getting Started         §5   5 questions
 *   04 MOQ & Order Size        §5   4 questions
 *   05 Suppliers & Sourcing    §5   6 questions
 *   06 Quality & Inspection    §5   5 questions
 *   07 Purchasing & Orders     §5   4 questions
 *   08 Shipping                §5   7 questions
 *   09 Pricing & Payment       §5   2 questions
 *   10 Custom Sourcing         §12  informational block, not a ninth category
 *   11 Final CTA               §13
 *                              33 questions in total
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
 *   · It does not rewrite the brief's answers. §5 of the brief calls them "the
 *     final page content" and forbids turning them into generic SEO copy; they
 *     are reproduced verbatim in `src/data/faq-page.js`, which also records the
 *     two places where a site-wide naming decision overrides the brief's
 *     literal wording.
 *   · It does not add a single fact. No customer count, supplier count, year
 *     count, country count, percentage, certification, award or guarantee
 *     appears anywhere on it — brief §16 names every one of those as forbidden.
 *     The answers that touch a limit say so plainly ("it cannot eliminate every
 *     sourcing risk", "it cannot guarantee that every possible defect … will be
 *     detected"), because those sentences are the brief's own.
 *   · It does not add a scripted accordion. The disclosure group is native
 *     `<details>`, so the whole page ships with no page-specific JavaScript and
 *     every answer is present in the served HTML — which is also why the
 *     category navigation can be plain fragment links (see `FaqCategories`).
 *   · It does not add a filter, a search box or a "was this helpful?" control.
 *     The brief's §1 lists six registers this page must not slide into, and an
 *     ecommerce/support-desk widget is one of them.
 *   · It does not redesign anything. Header, footer, hero, disclosure group,
 *     prose block and closing band are all the existing site's.
 *
 * INDEXABILITY
 *   The page is real, so it is indexable and appears in sitemap.xml. Its entry
 *   in `reservedTopLevel` (src/data/routes.js) was removed in the same change —
 *   that list drives the noindex/sitemap split, so forgetting it would have
 *   left a finished, content-rich page excluded from search.
 *
 * STRUCTURED DATA
 *   WebPage + BreadcrumbList + FAQPage, all built from the same records the page
 *   renders. The FAQPage node is the one thing on this page that is new to the
 *   site: `schema.js` had no FAQ builder and no page emitted one, so the node is
 *   deliberately used by `/faq` alone and the builder's docblock records why the
 *   other FAQ-bearing pages were left out and what extending it would involve.
 *
 * IMPORT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING
 *   Layout first, then the shared sections, then this page's own sheet —
 *   otherwise these rules lose every same-specificity conflict against the
 *   shared core. `npm run audit` fails the build if that regresses.
 *
 *   `service-detail.css` IS REQUIRED, despite its name. It is the sheet that
 *   styles the shared SECTION DEVICES — `.sec-note`, `.sec-prose`, `.sec-review`,
 *   `.sec-checklist`, `.sec-split`, `.sec-related`, `.sec-process-dark` — which is why all
 *   nine other pages that render one of them import it (`/services`, the five
 *   `/services/<slug>` pages, `/industries`, `/how-it-works`, `/about`). §12 of
 *   this page renders `ServiceProse`, so omitting the sheet made that band fall
 *   back to `display: block`: a 1216px paragraph at an uncapped measure, with
 *   the two-column editorial split silently gone. Nothing in the markup changes
 *   — only the geometry — which is exactly the class of defect a build-and-read
 *   review does not catch.
 *
 * WHY A SECTION DISPATCHER
 *   The page's eleven bands have three shapes (a navigation, seven category
 *   disclosure groups, a prose block) and the brief fixes their order. A
 *   dispatcher keeps that order, the ground of each band and all of its copy in
 *   `src/data/faq-page.js`, so a band can be reordered without touching markup
 *   and an unknown `type` throws at build time rather than silently rendering
 *   nothing. The hero and the closing band sit outside it because they are
 *   shared devices with their own prop sets.
 *
 *   `section.anchor` is passed as `titleId`, NOT as an id on the <section>: the
 *   jump list has to land on the category's heading, and landing on the section
 *   would scroll past its top padding and leave the heading far down the
 *   viewport. See `ServicesFaq`'s header note.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/services-shared.css';
import '../styles/service-detail.css';
import '../styles/faq.css';

import FinalCTA from '../components/FinalCTA.astro';
import FaqCategories from '../components/faq/FaqCategories.astro';
import ServiceProse from '../components/services/ServiceProse.astro';
import ServicesFaq from '../components/services/ServicesFaq.astro';
import ServicesHero from '../components/services/ServicesHero.astro';

import { breadcrumbs, faqItems, finalCta, hero, meta, sections } from '../data/faq-page.js';
import {
  breadcrumbItems,
  breadcrumbSchema,
  faqPageSchema,
  webPageSchema,
} from '../data/schema.js';

const path = '/faq';

/**
 * One graph for the page. The FAQPage node is built from `faqItems`, which
 * `faq-page.js` derives from the same `sections` this template maps over — so
 * the structured data cannot describe a question the page does not render.
 */
const schema = [
  webPageSchema({ title: meta.title, description: meta.description, path }),
  breadcrumbSchema(breadcrumbItems(breadcrumbs)),
  faqPageSchema(faqItems),
];
---

<BaseLayout title={meta.title} description={meta.description} path={path} preloadImageKey={hero.image.key} schema={schema}>
  <ServicesHero
    breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
    eyebrow={hero.eyebrow}
    title={hero.title}
    description={hero.description}
    primaryCta={hero.primaryCta}
    secondaryCta={hero.secondaryCta}
    imageKey={hero.image.key}
    imageCaption={hero.image.caption}
    titleId="fq-01"
  />

  {
    sections.map((section) => {
      switch (section.type) {
        case 'categories':
          /* §3. A band with a <nav> inside it — see the component for why it is
             not a heading, and why it is a section rather than a bare <nav>. */
          return (
            <FaqCategories items={section.items} label={section.label} tone={section.tone} />
          );

        case 'faq':
          /* The seven categories. `tone` alternates so no two adjacent bands
             share a ground; `anchor` becomes the heading's id, which is what
             the navigation above scrolls to. */
          return (
            <ServicesFaq
              eyebrow={section.eyebrow}
              title={section.title}
              items={section.items}
              tone={section.tone}
              titleId={section.anchor}
            />
          );

        case 'prose':
          /* §12 — the Custom Sourcing block. Deliberately NOT a ninth
             category: the brief asks for "a small informational block … rather
             than creating another large FAQ category". */
          return (
            <ServiceProse
              eyebrow={section.eyebrow}
              title={section.title}
              paragraphs={section.paragraphs}
              links={section.links}
              tone={section.tone}
              titleId={section.anchor}
            />
          );

        default:
          /* A typo in a `type` string must not render nothing and pass the
             build — the band would simply vanish from the page. */
          throw new Error(
            `[/faq] Unknown section type "${section.type}". ` +
              `See the section vocabulary in src/data/faq-page.js.`
          );
      }
    })
  }

  <FinalCTA
    eyebrow={finalCta.eyebrow}
    title={finalCta.title}
    description={finalCta.description}
    cta={finalCta.cta}
  />
</BaseLayout>
